 # Gradient-Constrained Load Balancing Loss
 
-Also called: LGC-load, balance-load loss
+Also called: LGC-load
 
 ## Overview
-Gradient-Constrained Load Balancing Loss (LGC-load) is a regularization term used during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting models to optimize computational efficiency by balancing the rendering workload across parallel pixel threads [S1]. It addresses the problem of uneven Gaussian distribution, where complex scene regions require more Gaussians for fidelity while simple regions do not, causing thread idle time in pixel-parallel rasterization [S1]. Unlike rigid load balancing that enforces uniform Gaussian counts, LGC-load adapts the allowed variance in Gaussian density per pixel based on local image gradients, thereby preserving reconstruction quality in intricate areas while reducing redundancy in simple areas [S1]. It builds on [Load Balancing for Pixel-parallel Splatting](8373-load-balancing-for-pixel-parallel-splatting.md).
